---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { manifest, gpuById, money, viewLabel } from '../lib/data';

const primary = manifest.primary_view;
const zones = manifest.zones[primary];
const t = manifest.normalization.transforms.find((x) => `${x.resolution}_${x.mode}` === primary) ?? manifest.normalization.transforms[0];
const names = Object.fromEntries(Object.values(gpuById).map((g) => [g.gpu_id, g.display_name]));
// The constant-ratio method for comparison on the same overlap set.
let constantRatio: number | undefined;
if (t) {
  const ratios = t.overlap_points.map((p) => p[2] / p[1]).sort((a, b) => a - b);
  const lo = Math.floor(ratios.length * 0.1);
  const hi = Math.floor(ratios.length * 0.9);
  const trimmed = hi > lo ? ratios.slice(lo, hi) : ratios;
  constantRatio = trimmed.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / trimmed.length;
}
const normalizedIds = Object.values(gpuById).filter((g) => Object.values(g.benchmarks).some((m) => Object.values(m).some((c) => c.normalized)));
const chartInput = t && {
  points: t.overlap_points,
  names,
  method: t.method,
  params: t.params,
  dropped: t.dropped_outliers,
  legacyRange: t.legacy_range,
  constantRatio,
};
---

<Base title="Methodology" description="How SiliconValueIndex turns benchmark FPS and retail prices into a cost-per-frame ranking, including cross-suite normalization.">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>How the numbers are made.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Review sites rank graphics cards by raw performance. A card that is 20% faster but 60% more expensive is not a better buy,
      just a more expensive one. This index ranks by what a frame costs.
    </p>

    <h2 id="score">The score</h2>
    <p>
      For each card and each view (a resolution and a rendering mode), <strong>cost per FPS = current price ÷ average FPS</strong>.
      Lower is better. There is no weighting and no opinion in the number. The default view is {viewLabel(primary)}.
    </p>
    <p>
      Value zones on the {viewLabel(primary)} board are fixed dollar thresholds: great under {money(zones.great_max, 0)},
      fair up to {money(zones.fair_max, 0)}, poor above that. Other views use percentile bands (the cheapest third, the middle third, the rest),
      because a frame at 4K costs more than a frame at 1080p and fixed thresholds would not mean the same thing.
    </p>

    <h2 id="benchmarks">Benchmarks</h2>
    <p>
      FPS comes from <a href="https://www.tomshardware.com/reviews/gpu-hierarchy,4388.html">Tom's Hardware's GPU benchmarks hierarchy</a>,
      which tests every card with the same games at the same settings. We use their rasterization and ray-tracing averages at
      1080p, 1440p and 4K "ultra". Upscaling and frame generation are not included.
    </p>

    <h2 id="normalization">Cards from an older test suite</h2>
    <p>
      Tom's replaces its game suite every couple of years and does not retest every old card. Newer games are heavier, so an
      older card's FPS from the old suite would look inflated next to a current card's FPS from the new suite.
    </p>
    <p>
      To compare them we look at the cards that were tested in both suites and fit a straight line from old-suite FPS to
      current-suite FPS. Cards that only exist in the old suite are translated along that line and marked <span class="badge">est.</span>
      on every page. Cards present in the current suite always use their measured number.
    </p>
    {t && (
      <>
        <p>
          Current fit for {viewLabel(primary)}, {t.from_suite} to {t.to_suite}:
          {t.method === 'linear' ? (
            <>
              <code>current = {(t.params.a as number).toFixed(3)} × older {(t.params.b as number) < 0 ? '−' : '+'} {Math.abs(t.params.b as number).toFixed(1)}</code>
            </>
          ) : (
            <code>current = {(t.params.scale as number).toFixed(3)} × older</code>
          )}
          , fitted on {t.n_used} of {t.n_overlap} overlapping cards{t.r2 != null && <>, R² {t.r2.toFixed(2)}</>}.
          {t.dropped_outliers.length > 0 && (
            <> Excluded as outliers: {t.dropped_outliers.map((id) => names[id] ?? id).join(', ')}.</>
          )}
        </p>
        <figure>
          <canvas id="overlap" height="360" aria-label="Scatter of overlapping cards with the fitted line"></canvas>
          <figcaption>
            Each point is a card tested in both suites. The solid line is the transform in use; the dashed line is the single
            ratio ({constantRatio?.toFixed(3)}) the index used before, which over-credited slower cards and under-credited faster ones.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </>
    )}
    <p>
      Cards currently shown with estimated FPS ({normalizedIds.length}):
      {normalizedIds.map((g, i) => (
        <>
          <a href={`/gpu/${g.gpu_id}/`}>{g.display_name}</a>{i < normalizedIds.length - 1 ? ', ' : '.'}
        </>
      ))}
    </p>

    <h2 id="prices">Prices</h2>
    <p>
      Each card's price is the lowest valid retail price we can find for a new, in-stock unit. A price is rejected when it is for a
      bundle, a laptop part, an open-box or refurbished unit, or when it falls outside 35% to 300% of that card's recent median
      (a sign of a mislabelled listing). Every price shows its retailer and the date it was checked. Prices are stored as history,
      so each card page shows how it has moved.
    </p>

    <h2 id="limits">What this does not tell you</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Averages hide game-specific behaviour. A card can be great value on average and weak in the one game you play.</li>
      <li>Memory matters beyond FPS. An 8 GB card can post a good average today and struggle in newer titles.</li>
      <li>Power draw, noise, size and features (encoders, upscaling quality) are not in the score. They are listed on each card's page.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The pipeline, data and this site are open source: <a href="https://github.com/SiliconValueIndex/siliconvalueindex">github.com/SiliconValueIndex/siliconvalueindex</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <script is:inline type="application/json" id="svi-overlap" set:html={JSON.stringify(chartInput ?? null)} />
</Base>

<script>
  import { drawOverlapChart } from '../lib/charts';
  const el = document.getElementById('svi-overlap');
  const canvas = document.getElementById('overlap') as HTMLCanvasElement | null;
  const input = el && JSON.parse(el.textContent || 'null');
  if (input && canvas) drawOverlapChart(canvas, input);
</script>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--t1);
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
